Don't have time today to finish up a PR, but here's a diff of what I've done, which appears to work fine.

I can fix the issue in my game by increasing the wait time beyond a hard-coded 1000ms to essentially indefinite. The vulkan and D3D12 drivers seem both to use a similar approach, so I don't think it's out of line to match that.

But even more importantly, there's another bug that exists regardless of the Metal timeout value. The problem is that `_wait_for_transfer_worker` doesn't grab the return value of `driver->fence_wait(p->command_fence);`:

```cpp
void RenderingDevice::_wait_for_transfer_worker(TransferWorker *p) {
    driver->fence_wait(p->command_fence);   // return value ignored!
    driver->command_pool_reset(p->command_pool);
    ...
}
```

So if `fence_wait` ever returns something other than `OK` (for any reason), the caller resets the command pool while the GPU is still using resources from it. This causes corrupt state, I think.

So, the wrapper I wrote does a couple things:

1. retries on ERR_TIMEOUT up to n times, so timeouts will just wait until slow GPU work finishes
2. if it does fail, skips the pool reset and logs an error so the engine crashes rather than silently corrupting state, and with an error that will help debug the issue

This isn't Metal-specific; Vulkan and D3D12 don't currently time out since they don't have that short `1000ms` timeout, but they would also silently discard errors of any type and probably corrupt state. So this should fix against theoretical problems with all 3 of the drivers.

Shouldn't add much, if any, overhead. The vast majority of the time, `fence_wait` should succeed on first call and exit the loop immediately. This is just a more failure resistant approach.

If you patch Godot and then run the patched version, you should see the problem disappear when running the repro project I linked above.

I have two patches, one for 2.6.x, and the other for 2.7.x, since there was a change from Obj-C++ to C++.

### Patch for 4.6.x:

