The main difference (other than the detailed logging) is that I moved the retry from the driver into the caller, and it doesn't give up. Just bumping up fence_wait to UINT32_MAX wasn't right. It would hang silently, which was even worse than before. Skipping the pool reset and returning wasn't right either. The logging really helped narrow it down.

Another problem was that the fences sometimes never signal. macOS would quarantine the process (`kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorSubmissionsIgnored`) after a couple of GPU faults and then we're locked out.

There was also another thing I encountered, where `MDImmediateLibrary::_check_and_wait` would freeze the main thread forever if the `MTLCompilerService` callback didn't arrive, which could happen even without GPU faults. This could _possibly_ explain a lot of the other reports which are around the metal fence wait freeze happening after upgrading, because it seemed to happen with cache rebuilding, maybe.

Does this all make sense to you? I mean, I guess the main thing is that I'm unblocked on my end. I just apply this and compile from scratch and I'm good. But I figured I'd share with everyone else too.

